  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Time Shifts Caused by Phase",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert the phase term in a continuous-time sinusoid into its equivalent time advance or delay using the angular frequency and the correct sign convention.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts Caused by Phase",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Shifts Caused by Phase",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A phase term inside a sinusoid is another way of saying the waveform happens earlier or later in time. Start from the zero-phase case $x(t)=\\cos(\\omega t)$, where there is no horizontal shift yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now add phase to the argument. If the signal is $x(t)=\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$, factor out $\\omega$ so the phase becomes part of the time variable instead of staying as a separate angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\cos\\left(\\omega\\left(t+\\frac{\\phi}{\\omega}\\right)\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That rewrite means a positive phase advances the signal by $\\phi/\\omega$ seconds. If you write shifts as $x(t-\\tau)$, then the same positive phase corresponds to $\\tau=-\\phi/\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\cos(\\omega t-\\phi)=\\cos\\left(\\omega\\left(t-\\frac{\\phi}{\\omega}\\right)\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A negative phase does the opposite: it delays the signal by $\\phi/\\omega$ seconds, and in $x(t-\\tau)$ notation that is $\\tau=\\phi/\\omega$. The same conversion works for sine signals too, so later examples can switch between $\\cos$ and $\\sin$ without changing the rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The target move is always the same: identify $\\omega$, divide the phase by $\\omega$, and read the result as a time advance or delay with the correct sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts Caused by Phase",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Rewriting a Positive Phase Term as a Time Advance",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $x(t)=2\\cos(8t+\\pi/2)$ as a cosine with no explicit phase term and identify the time advance.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the positive phase term in $x(t)=2\\cos(8t+\\pi/2)$. Keep the cosine and the amplitude $2$, and rewrite only the time argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the angular frequency is $\\omega=8$ and the phase is $\\phi=\\pi/2$. The advance amount comes from dividing the phase by the angular frequency: $\\Delta t=\\phi/\\omega=\\pi/16$ seconds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "8t+\\pi/2=8\\left(t+\\pi/16\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That rewrite shows the signal is the same cosine shifted left by $\\pi/16$ seconds. In shift notation, this is a time advance, so the waveform appears earlier than $2\\cos(8t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2\\cos\\left(8\\left(t+\\pi/16\\right)\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the final interpretation is a time advance of $\\pi/16$ seconds, and the amplitude stays $2$. The same divide-by-$\\omega$ rule is what later positive-phase examples will reuse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts Caused by Phase",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Rewriting a Negative Phase Term as a Time Delay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $x(t)=5\\sin(6t-\\pi/3)$ as a sine with no explicit phase term and identify the time delay.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the negative phase term in $x(t)=5\\sin(6t-\\pi/3)$. Keep the amplitude and sine form fixed, and rewrite only the angle so it looks like $\\omega(t-\\Delta t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $\\omega=6$ and $\\phi=-\\pi/3$, so the delay magnitude is $\\Delta t=|\\phi|/\\omega=(\\pi/3)/6=\\pi/18$ seconds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "6t-\\pi/3 = 6\\left(t-\\pi/18\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=5\\sin\\left(6\\left(t-\\pi/18\\right)\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A negative phase term becomes a right shift, so this signal is delayed by $\\pi/18$ seconds. The sine follows the same phase-to-time rule as cosine: use the sign to decide advance or delay, then use $|\\phi|/\\omega$ for the amount.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts Caused by Phase",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Computing Shift Size from Phase and Angular Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "How far and in which direction is $x(t)=2\\cos(5t+3)$ shifted relative to $2\\cos(5t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the positive phase term in $x(t)=2\\cos(5t+3)$. Keep the cosine and amplitude $2$ fixed, and rewrite only the angle so it looks like $\\omega(t+\\Delta t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $\\omega=5$ and $\\phi=3$, so the shift size is $\\Delta t=\\phi/\\omega=3/5$ seconds. The units work because radians divided by radians per second give seconds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5t+3=5\\left(t+3/5\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2\\cos\\left(5\\left(t+3/5\\right)\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal is advanced by $3/5$ seconds relative to $2\\cos(5t)$. The same divide-by-$\\omega$ rule used in the earlier phase examples still applies when the phase is a plain number instead of a multiple of $\\pi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts Caused by Phase",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Phase-Caused Shifts in Sine Signals",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Interpret $x(t)=3\\sin(7t-2)$ as a time shift and rewrite it without an explicit phase term.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the negative phase term in $x(t)=3\\sin(7t-2)$. Keep the sine form and amplitude $3$ fixed, and rewrite only the angle so it looks like $\\omega(t-\\Delta t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $\\omega=7$ and $\\phi=-2$, so the delay magnitude is $\\Delta t=|\\phi|/\\omega=2/7$ seconds. The units work because radians divided by radians per second give seconds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "7t-2 = 7\\left(t-2/7\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3\\sin\\left(7\\left(t-2/7\\right)\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal is delayed by $2/7$ seconds relative to $3\\sin(7t)$. The same phase-to-time rule used for cosine applies to sine as well: identify $\\omega$, divide the phase by $\\omega$, and read the sign as an advance or delay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
